        "geology": "The small low mountain range of Kellerwald consists of about 300 to 400 million year old marine deposits which were folded into mountains.\r\nThe base rocks of the National Park are ancient shale and Greywacke (Carboniferous / Devonian). Particularly the northern slopes and cliff bottoms are fillded with alluvial sediments, the wider valleys have Loess-loam sediment. Locally one finds siliceous shale, and in seams diabase (greenstone).\r\nParticularly the northern slopes and cliff bottoms are fillded with alluvial sediments, the wider valleys have Loess-loam sediment.\r\nThe acidic basic rocks create mainly nutrient poor non-basic soils.",
        "hydrology": null,
        "soils": "The varied relief of the National Park is again reflected in the mosaic of the soil types. The acidic base rocks (Greywacke, clay slate and siliceous slate) have given the National Park predominantly medium depth brown earth with medium to low base saturation.\r\nOn hillsides and in valley floors, as well as on some saddles, a layer of loess loam and loess-containing colluvium is found here and there. Deeper, nutrient\r\nricher brown earths have developed here, with noticeably luxuriant vegetation, especially in spring. On dry slopes and hilltops, shallow brown earth and ranker have developed. In this nutrient- and water poor area, beech-oak-dry woods mostly grow, often with acid and infertile soil indicator species in the herb layer. The raw soil types like syrosem or regosol are found on extremes of cliff or scree areas. Due to the barren situation, often only specially adapted mosses and lichens can live, and trees rarely grow.\r\nIn the broader stream valleys, more nutrient and base rich alluvial soils have developed, e.g. gleys. In spring areas and in humid hollows, higher ground\r\nwater produces gleys and pseudogleys. Under particularly oxygen poor, waterlogged conditions, a mosaic of half-bog gley soils develops.",
        "vegetation": "In the National Park typical plants of deciduous forests are enriched by rare and specialised species of dry and humid biotopes from natural and cultivated\r\nlandscapes. Up to now 635 ferns and flowering plants has been verified in the National Park.\r\nDetailed surveys, including more than 1100 species, are now also available for fungi. Primarily for fungi colonising wood, numerous rarities and new\r\ndiscoveries can be denoted ? it is expected to be about more than 1.500 species."
      },
